<!--
  Three stacked weight bars: the "open weights" mark.

  Lime on ink. It has been violet, then brass, and each move is argued in
  docs/design/visual-language.md; this one comes with the rest of the palette, and lime is
  the only chromatic note anywhere in the app now.

  One hue rather than three. A value ladder was tried and the third bar goes muddy against
  a dark tile at launcher size; measured at 96px, flat lime is the only version where all
  three bars still read.

  Geometry answers to the adaptive mask, and the arithmetic is not the obvious one. The x in
  each M is where the straight run starts, not where the bar starts: both arcs bulge a
  further 5.5 outward, so a bar drawn from 29 actually spans 23.5 to 79. Written that way
  once, the mark sat 5.5 left of centre and its top-left cap reached 35.2 from the middle of
  a viewport whose safe circle has a radius of 33, so a circular launcher mask would have
  clipped it. Starting at 34.5 puts the bars at 29 to 79, centred on 54, with the farthest
  point 30.7 and inside the circle every launcher shape is guaranteed to keep.

  That gives a longest bar of 50 in a 108 viewport, thickness 11, pitch 16. As fractions of
  the longest bar: 1, 0.7, 0.41 long, 0.22 thick, 0.32 apart. Those five numbers are the
  mark, and Mark.kt and play/graphics/mark.py draw it from the same five.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">
    <path
        android:fillColor="#E0FF4F"
        android:pathData="M34.5,32.5h39a5.5,5.5 0,0 1,0 11h-39a5.5,5.5 0,0 1,0 -11z" />
    <path
        android:fillColor="#E0FF4F"
        android:pathData="M34.5,48.5h24a5.5,5.5 0,0 1,0 11h-24a5.5,5.5 0,0 1,0 -11z" />
    <path
        android:fillColor="#E0FF4F"
        android:pathData="M34.5,64.5h9.5a5.5,5.5 0,0 1,0 11h-9.5a5.5,5.5 0,0 1,0 -11z" />
</vector>
